#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:5
#: 2dcd297030b742f89126cfb19be0bb20
msgid "Inverted Flight"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:7
#: 65bcd037c2b749c085b4de93c70f0397
msgid "Since helicopters, including dual rotor and heli-quads, are capable of generating negative thrust (negative collective) they have the capability of flying, even hovering, inverted. This, of course, requires that the vehicle is capable of generating sufficient thrust while inverted and is setup to do so (see :ref:`traditional-helicopter-aerobatic-setup`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:9
#: b21137315c6a4aad89b09c7caef1ca92
msgid "Inverted flight can be commanded in two different ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:11
#: 559eb010485543088fb58b78077f2729
msgid "Under pilot attitude control in ACRO mode which requires the pilot to push the collective stick down to climb while inverted and the pitch control is oriented to the inverted aircraft as well, or"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:12
#: aea9816a338e44528d972cf801d1baad
msgid "Under ArduPilot attitude stabilization control, in STABILIZE, ALTHOLD, LOITER, and AUTO modes using the ``RCx_OPTION`` invert switch function \"43\". This merely flips the effective orientation of the IMU to be inverted and the vehicle follows. It will stabilise the vehicle with a roll of 180 degrees from normal whenever inverted flight is enabled. While inverted, collective is used normally in STABILIZE, ALTHOLD and LOITER where pushing up on the collective causes the inverted helicopter to climb away from the ground.  Pitch control is also used as though the aircraft is upright."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:15
#: a0ce1a13f2004dbbbb556e8596ab0236
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:17
#: 0f515bad248a4c26ad52f9d36c7907fd
msgid "GPS required modes such as LOITER and AUTO require good GPS performance which may be impacted adversely if the GPS is facing down for long periods, especially hover precision. Dual blended GPSes oriented such that one has a \"sky-view\" while inverted can ameliorate this. In extreme cases, EKF failsafes might occur with undesirable effects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:18
#: b8b101a1d85048798cad5566e0ed37ea
msgid "Negative collective range is not impacted using the invert switch, ie it becomes the effective climb control so be sure there is sufficient range."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:21
#: acfefc71919a4e60b119e069ab737895
msgid "Setup of the inverted flight feature"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:23
#: ac0a85d3258445d2a7b3b3a254505aad
msgid "The inverted flight feature is initiated by setting an RC channel via RCx_OPTION parameter.  The RCx_OPTION parameter allows you to configure a transmitter switch to enable inverted flight for helicopters. To enable it, choose a RC input channel which is not being used for anything else, and setup your transmitter to give a PWM value larger than 1750 when a previously unused 2 position switch is enabled, and a value below 1200 when it is not enabled.  For example, if channel 7 is currently unused, and you set :ref:`RC7_OPTION<RC7_OPTION>` to InvertedFlight (which has value 43) then if your transmitter sends 1900 on channel 7 the helicopter will flip upside down and continue flying in whatever mode it is in, but with the helicopter inverted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:25
#: ce36096df3bb45abbd380af5b812ff72
msgid "Be sure to setup pitch and roll rate limits.  This will keep the attitude controller from commanding unachievable roll or pitch rates.  It is recommended in STABILIZE or ACRO that you command max roll and pitch rates.  Pull the flight logs and determine what is the maximum pitch and roll rate the aircraft can achieve.  Then set :ref:`ATC_RATE_R_MAX<ATC_RATE_R_MAX>` for max roll rate and :ref:`ATC_RATE_P_MAX<ATC_RATE_P_MAX>` for max pitch rate.  If you are unable to determine the max rates then be conservative and set them to 120 deg/s initially."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:28
#: a93bdd3d881c45e39879a0336c1f83a2
msgid "Tips"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:30
#: 532888716ad94710a1e9da65a8269905
msgid "Here are some general tips about inverted flight"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:32
#: b16083493f47458aabc2a49692aeef43
msgid "Try it out in simulation first to get used to it, and ensure your transmitter is setup right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/traditional-helicopter-inverted-flight.rst:34
#: 776c3a38db754c1596bd8ae7ee207883
msgid "make sure you have plenty of height before engaging or disengaging inverted flight for the first time. The aircraft should not lose much altitude during the transition but it is worth having the extra altitude in case something unexpected happens.  As you get more comfortable in the aircraft's ability to transition from upright to inverted and vice-versa, then you can lower the altitude where the transition is made."
msgstr ""
